A *beta* release of the SQUISH audio compression/decompression utility is now
available via anonymous ftp from DeskFish.mit.edu in the /pub/compression_apps
directory.  Binaries are available for Linux, Dec Ultrix, RS6k AIX and Sun4.

The SQUISH faq is attached below.  Please note that the beta release does not yet
have the -i -H or -w options enabled.  These option will follow in another
release shortly; for the moment, however, this means SQUISH is limited to 16 bit
raw sample data.

Monty

****INCLUDED FILE FOLLOWS****

SQUISH v0.0 Audio Compression Frequently Asked Questions and
Propaganda List

Last updated: Thu Aug 25 15:46:16 EDT 1994

1) What is SQUISH?

SQUISH is a compression package designed to reduce the file size of
digitized 8 and 16 bit audio samples (or samples of any periodic
data).  SQUISH will operate on files sampled at any speed, but it is
designed to work with very high quality samples, for example, CD
quality samples.

2) Why another compression?  There's already GZIP, MPEG, Mulaw, etc...

Normal compression utilities such as UNIX 'compress' and 'gzip', and
DOS 'PKZIP' are virtually useless for compressing digitized sound
files.  gzip -9 will usually reduce file size of digitized sound by
about 10%.

The audio compression formats that already exist each have drawbacks
that limit their usefulness in general purpose sound compression.  For
example, Mulaw is very low quality and usually limited to low sample
speeds.  MACE is also designed for lower quality samples; MPEG usually
only goes to 6:1, and is *only* lossy.  Other compression techniques
(such as AT&T's recently announced format, the name of which escapes
me) are optimized for voice or some other specific kind of sound.

SQUISH is designed to compress any sound without sound quality loss at
moderately high ratios.  A CD quality sample compressed at the highest
lossy ratios will take up less than 70% as much space as an 8 bit 8kHz
mulaw sample while preserving the entire frequency and dynamic range
of a CD.

3) Is SQUISH a lossless compression?
	
SQUISH will work both as a lossy or lossless compression, depending on
user selectable command line options.  Lossy compression will result
in a smaller files than lossless at the expense of sound quality
(Lossless settings up to -5 should not produce any audible artifacts
or distortion).  SQUISH is designed to lose as little information as
possible while performing lossy compression.

4) What sound file formats will SQUISH compress?

SQUISH requires the sound file to be 8 or 16 bit (signed or unsigned)
raw data, sampled at any rate.  Input files may be little or big
endian (regardless of platform), and can contain up to 256 interleaved
channels.

5) Can SQUISH compress any other file types (ie, Mulaw, .WAV, etc.)?
	
Yes.  Although SQUISH is designed to compress raw data, the -w option
will compress sound files that consist of raw linear data with a short
(<50 byte) header, such as most .WAV files.

Mulaw files must be converted to linear data before compression.  The
sound utility SOX was designed expressly for the purpose of conversion
and will convert virtually all known file formats to raw data and
back.  Using SOX is fairly easy (and fast), and constructing a few
scripts to run SOX on (for example) an .au file then pipe the output
into SQUISH for compression will achieve the desired effect.  SOX
binaries and source code are freely available from quite a few FTP
sites (ie, wuarchive.wustl.edu, sunsite.unc.edu, switch.nic.ch, etc.)

If the input file *has* been converted from anything other than
another linear format, the -H option should be used in order to alert
SQUISH that the input file has fewer discrete input values than a
normal 16 bit file.  SQUISH will then be able to properly compress out
the 'extra' space.

6) How small will files get?

Using the -5 option (the default option), files will compress anywhere
from minimum of 1.5:1 to 3:1, depending on the type of sound being
compressed.  Typically, 2:1 can be expected; these ratios are
comparable to the ratios that LZW (the format used by PKZIP and gzip)
gets on text and binary files.  The Eroica Symphony, for example,
compressed with a ratio of 2.66:1 lossless when using -5.

Lossy compression will always get better ratios than lossless, even
using the -10 option.  Using the -l10 option, lossy will get between
14:1 and 16:1 depending on the type of sound being compressed.
Obviously, samples containing silence will be much smaller.

It is safe to assume that SQUISH will almost always be able to deliver
5:1 with no perceptible change in sound quality.

7) How fast is SQUISH?  Will it compress realtime?

SQUISH must make more than one pass over a sound file in order to
compress it; for this reason, it is not suited to realtime
compression.  SQUISH can decompress in one pass, however, and
decompression will be fast enough to occur realtime during playback,
once the beta code is optimized.  Especially relevant to Linux users,
a math coprocessor of some flavor is fairly important; SQUISH does the
majority of its work in floating point.  The good news is that my
486DX50 outperforms a SPARC IPC and IBM RS6000 320H when running
SQUISH.  I can't wait to try it on a Pentium :)

7) How does SQUISH work?

SQUISH uses a veritable cocktail of compression techniques; both old
(huffman trees and RLE, for example) and new variants of older ideas
(Bitstreak, MEM, FFT), as well as anything that would make files
smaller without introducing (much) distortion or noise.  Rumor has it
that even the Colonel's Secret Recipe (salt, black pepper, monosodium
glutamate) makes a guest appearence in order to give the entire
compression package a crispy, yet tasty coating of breading.

The compression techniques used by SQUISH are not derived from any
work done by MPEG or AT&T (unless signal theory developed at Bell labs
in the '30s and '40s is considered).

7) What platforms will SQUISH run on?

SQUISH is currently compiled for Linux (i386/486/Pentium), IBM AIX
(RS/6000), DEC Ultrix (DECstation), and SUN Solaris (SPARCstation).
Programmers who are willing to help port to new platforms are welcome.
I already have a volunteer for the PowerMac... 

8) Is development continuing? Is SQUISH likely to improve?
	
Yes.  The biggest problem with development has been a surplus (not a
dearth) of ideas...  I had a working package in February, but there
was always one more thing to try...

Specifically, the following improvements are immediately in the works:

1) The storage of frequency domain impulse amlitudes is not as
efficient as it could be; the floating point representation being used
'wastes' zeroes.

2) The algorithm currently used for determining the amount of the
audio spectrum currently being used is easily fooled; this results in
audible artifacts at high lossy settings, and less than optimal
compression ratios at all lossy settings..  A new algorithm is being
coded.

3) SQUISH is not terribly memory efficient in terms of time or space;
this is not a problem with the algorithms, but the code.  As the code
is cleaned up, speed and memory requirements should improve.

8) Is SQUISH free?  Where do I get it?

SQUISH is free just like virtually everything else (originally) for
Linux.  At the moment, SQUISH lives primarily on my own machine,
deskfish.mit.edu, where it is available via anonymous ftp (hopefully
it will propogate to popular ftp sites; DeskFish is my development
machine, and it doesn't need the load).  Binaries for various machines
are compiled in the /pub/compression_apps directory.  Source will be
generally available as soon as I get it cleaned up a bit (once SQUISH
goes from Beta to release).

Unlimited personal and non-commercial use of SQUISH is encouraged.
Commercial use of SQUISH binaries or source code in any form requires
formal liscensing arrangements; this includes third-party software
